Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:02.070 align:start position:0%
 
you're watching the daily climate show  on sky news  on today's programme wildfires continue  to rage across  southern europe with hot dry weather  conditions set to stay  oxfam warns that an over-reliance on  tree planting by governments to meet  their net zero carbon targets  could cause food prices to jump  and dangers in the deep a new study  shows marine life in the caribbean  is being contaminated by single-use  plastics  [Music]  hello and welcome to the uk's only daily  climate news show where we have  continuing coverage  of the extreme heat wave that's hit  parts of the mediterranean  and in particular the wildfires that  have been caused by the extreme heat  we start with turkey where fires are  continuing to rage across the country  extreme heat conditions have made it  easier for the fires to ignite  and to spread well authorities there  have launched an international appeal  to help tame the fires that have killed  eight people in recent days  thousands of people have been forced to  flee their homes in the south of the  country  with coastal areas amongst the worst  affected  well our special correspondent alex  crawford is in turkey larry in turkey  where firefighters  are trying to prevent the fire from  spreading  there are still lots of pockets of fires  which they're  trying desperately to dampen and put out  with fairly limited success you can see  they're sweeping the fire is sweeping  across the forest  mountains and every time they put out  one lot or try and dampen it down it  moves across  the winds don't help it at all and what  they're trying to do is prevent  the fire from encroaching onto these  tourist  buildings they've been evacuated there's  no one in there but they're trying to  prevent  the loss of of of these buildings and of  the power station which is just a few  kilometers away they have got  some air support but they're urgently  asking for more it's patently  inadequate and there's a lot of anger  about why it's taking so long to bring  all of these fires  under control of fresh ones are breaking  out  all the time we're now in the seventh  day  and if you thought climate change didn't  affect you  then this is probably proof it does with  temperatures soaring and expected to  rise even  more over the next few days and  certainly  long into the week they're saying that  humidity is very low  the forest here is very very  exceptionally dry because of these  exceptional  temperatures and these conditions are  proving to be extremely challenging for  all the firefighters and the authorities  and it's not just turkey that's feeling  the impact of this intense heat  temperatures have reached above 40  degrees celsius in parts of greece with  fires spreading  there too authorities have described the  heat wave as the most intense in more  than  30 years and have advised people to  avoid direct sunlight around  midday well our euro correspondent  michelle clifford is in pacinthos  in rhodes where it's becoming  increasingly inhospitable for residents  and for tourists  well clearly you get high temperatures  in greece over the summer that's part of  the reason people come here  on holiday that this is being called the  worst heat wave in over 30 years because  they've been  persistently high temperatures over a  long period of time  and those temperatures coupled with  strong winds have  created a toxic mix for firefighters and  the military trying to contain  forest blazes now the priority is to  stop those blazes  reaching residential areas but this week  some villages had to be evacuated we  spoke  to some of the residents forced out of  their homes they told us they have  noticed  changes over the summer summer period  things getting hotter  in recent years they ask them why they  think that's happening they said climate  change  they acknowledge that they have a role  to play in uh  making things better reducing their  energy consumption but some said  uh the big polluters have to be taken in  hand by the government now  the greek development minister was here  today he said  climate change absolutely has to be  addressed if we're not going to see  these sorts of events repeated summer  after summer  in today's other climate news saint  lucia's prime minister  philip pierre is urging rich nations to  ramp up climate  finance to caribbean states vulnerable  to hurricanes  the pandemic has hit the caribbean's  tourism-dependent economies  meaning there's less money available to  the islands to build homes that can  withstand the extreme weather  scientists warn global warming is  causing severe hurricanes to become more  frequent  and out of season  changing sea levels can affect how often  volcanic eruptions occur on a greek  island according to a study published  in nature geoscience analyzing the  timings of 36  000 eruptions researchers found there  were more volcanic eruptions when sea  levels fell below  40 meters over half of the world's  volcanoes are islands or coastal  and rising sea levels could put the  populations there at risk  from eruptions and a contest to preserve  the pyramid of the feathered serpent  from climate change has been announced  in mexico  air pollution and acid rain has caused  the pyramid to deteriorate  so entrants have been asked to design a  roof structure that can protect the  ancient monument  the mexican institution of history and  anthropology who launched the contest  say climate change is forcing them to  take drastic measures to conserve the  ruins  governments and businesses have made  commitments to significantly reduce  their greenhouse gas emissions  with many promising to reach net zero in  the coming years  well the new report by oxfam has found  too many of these promises  rely on planting trees and restoring  forests on land  that's needed for crops to feed a  growing population  it estimates it could push global food  prices up by 80 percent  by 2050. well here to explain more is  danny srikanda raja chief executive of  oxfam  gb uh welcome to you so explain to us a  bit more the downsides of using  tree planting as a way to reach net zero  goals what are your concerns  well as you know and your viewers will  know there are lots of upsides to using  trees but the problem we've calculated  is that  once you total up the sort of net zero  commitments that big  companies and country or governments are  making  you end up with a ludicrous total of the  land that's going to be needed  in new forests and our estimate is that  it could need as much as 1.6  billion hectares between now and 2050  of new forests in order to meet the net  zero targets i mean that's the  area of five times uh india for example  and it's equivalent  to the amount of arable farmland that's  in use today  and so if you know that sounds to us  like a ludicrous amount of  land that's just impossible to reach and  of course even if we came  close to meeting those sorts of targets  the problem may be  that uh we'll be using valuable precious  farmland and  and and planting that with trees with  forests rather than using it to grow the  food that the world population will need  and  you know we're already seeing rising  hunger rising food prices  and if there's a sort of land grab for  carbon sinks for new forests  uh this is not going to help in terms of  the fight against poverty  and a lot of people say it's a vital  tool in  reducing carbon emissions because trees  suck in carbon and they're also really  good for biodiversity as well  so what are you suggesting as an  alternative well i think just to  just to clarify here that you know it is  a vital tool but it doesn't seem quite  right that the world's  largest oil and gas companies for  example continue to exploit  new fossil fuel uh fields you know a  plan to be pumping out new carbon and  then are planning to offset that  with uh with new forests and that  doesn't seem right because  what we really need is urgent action to  reduce emissions  quickly dramatically it also means not  um  opening up new fields and new production  of oil and gas and so i think that's a  problem there  the other one of course is that a lot of  the most intense  ways of capturing carbon uh involve  large monoculture forests you know using  trees like eucalyptus or acacia and that  might be great at um  capturing carbon efficiently but it's  not necessarily  good for sustainable agriculture for  biodiversity  for community-based agroforestry which  might need a  different uh approach and so we're  really calling for urgent action to  look at exactly what lies behind the net  zero targets  and of course if it includes forestation  that's great but let's also be sensible  about how we approach  that aforestation as well dani  sriskandar raja thanks very much indeed  scientists are warning that marine life  in the caribbean is at risk of being  contaminated by plastics and other  man-made fibers in the sea the  all-female crew on board the research  vessel  expedition found a majority of these  plastics can be traced back to the  caribbean's tourism and fishing  industries  both vital to the island's economies our  correspondent ashna harinag reports  unsightly and welcome and for a postcard  destination  undesirable  plastics on the shores and seabeds of  caribbean islands  are threatening their futures  this is the finding of a pioneering  all-female crew of scientists  who have spent over two years analyzing  samples on land and sea  already see right here a pretty chunky  piece of white micro plastic and feels  really stiff it's got a jagged edge  that's the sharp eye of emily penn who  is leading the project  her team scoped out the plastic strewn  destinations to try and understand its  origins  a lot of what we were doing this year  was really trying to understand  the sort of chemistry of the plastic  that can then help inform where that  plastics come from because this  soup of micro plastic fragments can be  very anonymous  it doesn't sort of have a brand label  necessarily on it  and i think we were maybe expecting that  it would be very obvious where a lot of  the sources had come from  but they found more than one sector to  blame the fishing industry  maritime industry and also tourism are  very important in the caribbean  but it's a complex challenge because  these appear to be some of the sources  of the plastics that we're finding  their study hopes to encourage more  joined up solutions between  nations antigua for instance has banned  plastic bags  unlike neighbourings and kits this is a  single-use  plastic cutlery and some single-use  styrofoam glasses  the survival of these islands beauty  depends on mankind when every  drop of ocean water could be one plastic  bottle  bag and beach ball away from this  ashna harinac sky news well that's  everything from us for today  on tomorrow's show how climate change is  putting the ancient  world at risk of disappearing thanks for  watching and see you then

